### 1. Node.js / JavaScript (Best for your current setup)

Since you are already using **Node.js** with `ffmpeg-static` and `msedge-tts`, these are the most relevant libraries:

| Library | Purpose & Features |
| :--- | :--- |
| **[Remotion](https://www.remotion.dev/)** | **#1 framework for programmatic video**. Create videos using **React, HTML, CSS, Three.js**, and render them to MP4 via headless Chromium & FFmpeg. Supports audio syncing, transitions, and dynamic parameters. |
| **[fluent-ffmpeg](https://github.com/fluent-ffmpeg/node-fluent-ffmpeg)** | A fluent, easy-to-use Node.js wrapper around FFmpeg. Simplifies complex filters, transcoding, concatenations, audio-video merging, and subtitle burns. |
| **[ffmpeg-static](https://github.com/eugeneware/ffmpeg-static)** | Pre-compiled static FFmpeg binaries for Windows, Linux, and macOS without requiring system-level installations. |
| **[Editly](https://github.com/mifi/editly)** | Declarative command-line and Node.js video editing tool. Allows defining clips, audio tracks, transitions, text overlays, and Ken Burns effects via JSON/JS objects. |
| **[Motion Canvas](https://motioncanvas.io/)** | TypeScript-based programmatic vector animation engine with built-in timeline preview and video exporter (great for educational and technical videos). |
| **[@napi-rs/canvas](https://github.com/Brooooooklyn/canvas)** or **[skia-canvas](https://github.com/samizdatco/skia-canvas)** | Ultra-fast native Skia-based HTML5 Canvas implementation in Node.js for generating 60 FPS frames with text and shapes. |
| **[Puppeteer](https://pptr.dev/) / [Playwright](https://playwright.dev/)** | Headless browser automation. Capture DOM elements, web animations (CSS/GSAP), and take snapshots frame-by-frame to pipe into FFmpeg. |
| **[@ffmpeg/ffmpeg (ffmpeg.wasm)](https://ffmpegwasm.netlify.app/)** | WebAssembly port of FFmpeg. Allows video processing and conversion directly inside the browser or Node without native dependencies. |

